{"name":"Halcyon Green","hex":"#9baaa2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"halcyon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kingfisher whose nesting by the sea was said, in classical mythology, to cause the Gods to restrain the wind and waves.\n\nThe dead body of such a bird, said in Tudor times to act as a weather vane when hung from a beam.","The dead body of such a bird, said in Tudor times to act as a weather vane when hung from a beam."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to the halcyon or kingfisher.","Calm, undisturbed, peaceful, serene."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/halcyon"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Halcyon (genus)","blocks":[{"text":"Halcyon is a genus of the tree kingfishers, birds in the subfamily Halcyoninae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Woodland_Kingfisher_%28Halcyon_senegalensis%29_South_Africa.jpg/330px-Woodland_Kingfisher_%28Halcyon_senegalensis%29_South_Africa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halcyon_(genus)","wikidata":"Q371767"},{"title":"Halcyon (Ellie Goulding album)","blocks":[{"text":"Halcyon is the second studio album by English singer-songwriter Ellie Goulding, released on 5 October 2012, by Polydor Records. It was recorded at multiple studios across the United Kingdom and Canada, including locations in Wales, London, Brighton, and Montreal. She worked with several producers on the album, such as Jim Eliot, Starsmith, Billboard, Justin Parker, and Monsta, in addition to collaborating with artists Tinie Tempah and Calvin Harris."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e1/Ellie_Goulding_-_Halcyon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halcyon_(Ellie_Goulding_album)","wikidata":"Q3279484"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Ruddy_Kingfisher.jpg/500px-Ruddy_Kingfisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Ruddy_Kingfisher.jpg/1280px-Ruddy_Kingfisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Ruddy_Kingfisher.jpg/500px